// 欢迎加入kvspace世界
lib kvlang/spec/runtime语义/crash语义与inplace恢复 {
	r####"# crash 语义与 inplace 恢复

Layout decides, per function, from which step a crashed vthread may replay without changing
the result, and records it in `/lib/<func>/‥crash_resume` (`[]char/utf8`). Implementation:
`layout/src/crash_resume.rs` (module header lists the premises).

The record is advisory: runtimec does not consume it, and applying it needs four things
runtimec does not have today:

- a persisted resume pc per frame (there is one `‥pc` per vthread; frame paths use a
  process-local serial);
- a CALL commit that cannot skip the callee (the CALL exit commits the caller's pc past the
  call before the callee's first commit, so a crash in between skips the call);
- rebinding of locals that are not fixed width (strings, arrays, containers) on a fresh process;
- clearing the frame directory before `entry`.

## Step model

- Every lowered instruction of the function is one step (step i is stored at `[i+1,·]`).
- A crash may land after any subset of a step's writes and before its pc commit.
- Replaying from step a re-runs a, a+1, ..., c in order; frame locals survive the crash.

## Slots and classes

Each operand is mapped to classes:

| operand | class |
|---|---|
| any parameter, by name or `*[0,±k]` (read params are bound by address too) | `Params` (one class: callers may pass one storage twice) |
| absolute key `/a/b` | its own class (P2: does not alias a parameter; checked for callers in the same unit) |
| bare local | its own class; a local of a container or unknown type also touches `Heap` |
| member or subscript of any base (`m·k`, `m·*k`, `_1·v`, `p·k`, `/a·k`) | `Heap`, plus a read of the base and the keys |
| deref of a local, absolute key or `**param` | `Heap` and `Bare` |
| literal, label | ignored |

`Heap` aliases Params and every absolute key; `Bare` aliases every address-taken local: one
named by `kvspace/key·abs` / `resolveptr`, passed to a non-builtin callee, or copied from as a
container. When a same-unit caller passes an absolute key or a path, `Params` also aliases every
absolute key. An operand that cannot be parsed makes the step `unknown`.

## Op table

`OP_TABLE` in `crash_resume.rs` records, per catalog row, what its runtimec handler does:
which read operand it writes in place, whether its write slots are replaced whole or only
patched or conditionally written, and its Heap / volatile / opaque effects. A surface name
reaches its rows through `rwir_alias_table.def` (specializations included). Examples:

- `compactarray·set(a, i, v) -> b` patches `a` and never writes `b`;
- `string·set` on utf32 patches 4 bytes of its write slot; `kvspace·listat` leaves the slot alone
  when the entry is absent; `compactarray·at` writes one element (whole only when the slot has
  the element type and that type is fixed width, so no `char/*`: layout lets a text result
  into a char slot);
- `random·*`, `time·now`, `vthread·pc` are volatile (replayed only as the crashed step itself);
  `vthread·create/run/call/setstatus`, `kvspace·watch`, `debugger` are opaque (never replayed);
- `println`, `print`, `cerr` read their operands and write stdout only; replay repeats them.

Any other op, a catalog row without an entry, and every declared or extern rwir (this unit or
the store) are unverified: they may read and write any storage, all read operands may be
written in place, write slots are partial and may be stored either way, the step never joins a
range with a < c, and its function is not REPLAYABLE.

Reads of a step also include synthetic reads of partial write slots and of write slots the
callee may read: same-unit rwfunc that reads (or marks `inplace`) that write param; store rwfunc
or unresolved callee: every write slot. A callee that writes a read param in place (directly or
through its own calls) writes the matching argument of the call step; a store or unresolved
callee may write every argument. Calls to same-unit rwfuncs also carry the callee's
absolute-key and Heap reads/writes. A kill is a whole write by a verified op to a bare local or
absolute key whose slot type is a fixed-width scalar (not `char/utf8`) or a string; container
writes (`map·new` keeps old members, a container `=` binds a path), member writes, partial
writes and call writes are not.

## Slot size

`write_body` stores in place and fails unless the value has the bound slot's size;
`write_new_value` rebinds the slot at the value's size. The table records which one each op
uses (copy, string·set: either; text results, compactarray creates, map·new, struct·new,
key·abs/tostr/resolveptr, time·now: rebind; everything else: in place). An in-place op that
writes an absolute key or a member counts as either, since `write_body` creates a key unresolved
at encode time and every member. Every slot whose type is not fixed width (strings, `char/*`,
pointers, arrays, containers, unknown) also has a size class `Size(slot)`:

- an in-place store reads `Size(slot)` (so does an in-place patch of a read operand);
- a rebinding store writes it, and kills it for a bare local or absolute key;
- unverified, store and unresolved callees do both on every write slot;
- a unit callee does both on the write params it stores in place (`sizes_wparam`, transitive),
  writes the size of the others, and passes on its size reads and writes of absolute keys and
  Heap.

A step's own size read never meets its own size write when the step is one op, or a call whose
callee reads and writes that size only at one step; it still meets every other step's, and a
size the callee conflicts on stays unexempt under every alias of it. A size
read and write of Params, an absolute key or Heap at different steps counts as that class being
read and written for `entry` and REPLAYABLE.

## Replayable callees

REPLAYABLE(f): same-unit rwfunc, no unknown, volatile or unverified step, no absolute key and no
Heap both read and written, every callee REPLAYABLE, not on a call cycle. Verified ops are
REPLAYABLE except opaque ones; store rwfuncs and unresolved callees are not.

## Verdict per step c

Blocks start at step 0, at every label and after every goto/br/return. A range [a..c] inside
c's block is SAFE iff every step is known and calls only REPLAYABLE callees; for a < c it holds
no volatile or unverified step and reads no absolute key (or Heap) the function writes anywhere; and no class
is read before being killed in the range and then written in the range.

1. a = the largest SAFE start: a == c replays c itself; otherwise `c<-a`.
2. else, if f is REPLAYABLE and Params is not both read and written: `c<-entry` (re-run the
   frame from step 0 with fresh locals).
3. else, if f is REPLAYABLE: `c<-caller` (drop the frame, rewind the caller's pc to its call
   step, apply the caller's verdict for that step; the CALL exit already committed the caller's
   pc past the call). In a frame without a caller (vthread root, `vthread·call` target, init),
   or when the caller's function is NOT_RESUMABLE, `caller` means not resumable.
4. else the function is `NOT_RESUMABLE:<c>:<reason>`, reason = the absolute key, `heap`,
   `unknown`, `volatile:<op>`, `unverified:<op>` or `callee:<name>`. It covers the whole
   function; `<c>` only names the first step that fails.

Record: `ALWAYS_RESUMABLE` (every step case 1 with a == c),
`CONDITIONALLY_RESUMABLE:<c><-<a>|entry|caller,...`, or `NOT_RESUMABLE:<c>:<reason>`.

## Anchors

- `x:int64 = 1; x = x + 1; x -> /resume/y` => `CONDITIONALLY_RESUMABLE:1<-0`.
- `while (i < 10) { i = i + 1 }` => the increment is `<-entry` (the loop body is its own block).
- `*acc + *d -> *acc` => `ALWAYS_RESUMABLE`: lowering loads `*acc` into a temp first.
- `native_acc(*d) -> *acc` with a user `rwir native_acc` => `NOT_RESUMABLE:1:unverified:native_acc`;
  `(*acc)[0] = 9` (verified `compactarray·set` on a write param) => `0<-caller`.
- `t = a[0]; compactarray·set(a, 0, 9) -> b; x = x + t` => no range across the set (it writes `a`).
- `10 -> /tmp/g; add_to(5) -> /tmp/g` => `NOT_RESUMABLE:1:/tmp/g`.
- `rwfunc f(int64 $a) -> (*int64 $r)` reading `a` after writing `*r` => `caller`: `f(v) -> v`
  binds both to `v`.
- `m2 = m1; m1·b -> t; t + 1 -> m2·b` => no range across the member write (`m2` aliases `m1`).
- `k = random·intn(2)` inside any longer range => `NOT_RESUMABLE:<c>:volatile:random·intn`.

## Corpus

`tutorial/` + `runtimec/tests/kv/`: 297 files, 28 fail layout, 471 functions:
`ALWAYS` 224 / `CONDITIONALLY` 110 / `NOT` 137 (102 `callee:`, mostly stdlib rwfuncs outside the
unit, recursion and callees that are not REPLAYABLE; 24 `heap`; 9 absolute key; 1 `volatile:`;
1 `unverified:`). Earlier models gave 224 / 111 / 136, 229 / 114 / 128, 227 / 115 / 129,
249 / 122 / 100 and,
before the rewrite, 407 / 64 / 0. Reproduce from `layout/`:

```
KVLANG_CORPUS_DIR=<dir> cargo test --release --test crash_resume_corpus -- --ignored --nocapture
```
	"#### -> /lib/kvlang/spec/runtime语义/crash语义与inplace恢复
}
